{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"equivalent fraction equivalent fraction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md","fileName":"Equivalent Fraction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Equivalent Fraction","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Fractions and Decimal Forms]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Fraction in Lowest Terms","Cancellation Law for Fractions","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"equivalent fraction equivalent fraction","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md","fileName":"Equivalent Fraction.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Equivalent Fraction","heading":"Equivalent Fraction","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Equivalent fractions are different numerator-denominator forms that represent the same number. For a nonzero factor (k),\n\n$$\\frac{m}{n}=\\frac{mk}{nk}.$$\n\nFor example, (2/3=4/6=10/15). Multiplying both components by the same factor changes the partition used to name the point but not the point itself. Dividing both by a common factor reverses the process and leads toward a [[Fraction in Lowest Terms]]. The equality is justified by the [[Cancellation Law for Fractions]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Fractions and Decimal Forms","Fraction in Lowest Terms","Cancellation Law for Fractions","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equivalent Fraction.md","sourceType":"mar